  <h2>What is a load?</h2>

  <p>The load prepares the body for explosive movement.</p>

  <p>Explosive movement is difficult from a resting position.</p>

  <p>
    Before a sprinter leaves the blocks, a quarterback throws a pass, or a
    golfer begins the downswing, the body prepares to move.
  </p>

  <p class="emphasis">
    The body puts itself in a position to move explosively.
  </p>

  <p>Hitting is no different.</p>

  <p>
    A good load prepares the body to move explosively without sacrificing
    control.
  </p>

  <p>But preparation alone isn't enough.</p>

  <p>
    The load should also position the hitter for everything that follows.
  </p>

  <p>
    A good load should not make it more difficult to stay balanced. It should
    not interfere with an efficient firing sequence. It should not force the
    barrel into a compromised path.
  </p>

  <p>Instead, it should strengthen each of them.</p>

  <p>
    Every pillar builds on the one before it. The load is successful not only
    because it prepares movement, but because it prepares the right movement.
  </p>

  <p>
    The appearance of a load may vary from hitter to hitter. Some move more.
    Some move less. Some begin earlier. Others later.
  </p>

  <p>The objective remains the same.</p>

  <p>
    Prepare the body to move explosively while improving everything that comes
    next.
  </p>

  <p class="final-thought">
    A load is not extra movement.<br />
    It is purposeful preparation.
  </p>
</ChapterLayout>